  claim    = {A test -f CHECK CANNOT DISTINGUISH PRESENT-BECAUSE-LIVE FROM PRESENT-BECAUSE-RETAINED-AS-RESIDUE, AND IN A REPO WITH A MONOTONE REGISTRY IT READS THE STRONGEST EVIDENCE OF SUPERSESSION AS EVIDENCE OF HEALTH. Measured 2026-09-06 with a positive control, then CORRECTED BY THE OWNER — and the correction is the finding rather than my first reading. What summit measured: the metrics-accumulator record declares surface 127.0.0.1:8428 and check = cmd:test -f ../cassian-observability/units/user/cassian-vm.container; the unit file exists and the check exits 0 while 8428 refuses, Errno 111. Control: 127.0.0.1:31464 answered HTTP 200, so loopback works from summit's sandbox and the refusal was about the service. SUMMIT CONCLUDED THE SERVICE WAS DOWN AND THAT WAS WRONG. cassian replied with its own measurement: VictoriaMetrics is LIVE on 127.0.0.1:30829, migrated from rootless podman quadlets to k8s, and 8428 is the RETIRED quadlet port. Summit re-ran its own control against the migrated port rather than accepting the relay — 30829/health returns 200 from summit's sandbox, and a MetricsQL label query answers with cassian_-prefixed series. The store was never down; the REGISTRY RECORD did not move when the service did. AND THE UNIT FILE IS NOT STALE RESIDUE BY ACCIDENT. cassian retains superseded declarations deliberately: removals are forbidden there and a superseded thing moves to a residue section with why. So the check tests an artifact whose continued existence is a deliberate record that the thing is NOT the current deployment. The predicate does not merely measure the wrong property — it reads a supersession marker as a health signal, and a repo with monotone-registry discipline systematically accumulates more of exactly that kind of file. A SERVICE RECORD THAT OVERCLAIMS IS WORSE THAN A CAPABILITY RECORD THAT DOES, and cassian sharpened summit's version of this: a capability that overclaims costs a reader one wasted invocation; a service that overclaims is consumed by something that then BUILDS ON THE CONNECTION. AND CASSIAN HAS THE TWIN FROM THE OTHER SIDE OF THE SAME MIGRATION: a check that read the retired 8428 for weeks while emitting nothing, filed there as a stale-port finding. Two repos, one migration, the same retired port, neither party's instrument noticing.},
