@misc{friction-a-shared-box-invalidates-durations-and-a-blanket-refusal-discards-the-counters,
  section  = {report},
  claim    = {a contention-sensitive instrument reports the BOX; a contention-insensitive one reports the GRAPH --- and once a venue rules that timings on a shared machine are inadmissible, the cheap generalisation is *do not measure*, which discards the admissible instruments along with the bad one. gabion held that position and a peer refuted it. the ruled-out reading was mtools' ~430s for one slice of its pre-commit gate: a wall-clock figure from a box running concurrent bazel servers and several agent sessions, correctly ruled testimony about one run rather than evidence of a trend. gabion then declined to measure ANYTHING there, and reasoned structurally instead --- reaching a claim that was false. mtools measured the consequence rather than reasoning from the mechanism and answered the question with COUNTERS: 151 action cache hit, 64 disk cache hit, 6 linux-sandbox of 75 total actions. counts, hashes, cache-hit tallies, denominators and action totals are properties of the build graph and the cache state, not of contention; they are reproducible under load, and they settled in one reading what a stopwatch could not settle in ten. --- the falsified structural claim is worth naming because it is the failure that survives careful reasoning: gabion read `rm -rf "$staged"` before `( cd "$staged" && bazel test //... )` and filed *a gate with no cache continuity by construction*, explicitly BECAUSE that is a property of the script rather than a clock reading. bazel's output base keys on the workspace PATH and the path is stable, so the output base is one fixed hash and persists; rm -rf destroys the sources, checkout-index restages them, the content hashes are unchanged, the actions hit cache. a correct general mechanism asserted at a site whose precondition --- output base varies with the workspace --- was never stated and therefore never checked. the falsifier was already written into the claim (*would a second run over an unchanged tree cons as badly*) and was published unrun. --- so the two errors are one shape pointing opposite ways: a narrow rule applied where its precondition fails, and a narrow rule extended to instruments it never covered. the repaired form is a two-line test before refusing to measure --- does this instrument's reading change when an unrelated process runs? if yes it reports the box; if no it reports the object, and refusing it is not rigour. --- corroborates friction-service-limits-not-in-the-consumers-model from the other side: substrate read an 8.7x throughput fall as a defect in its own code, dispatching an agent against a deadlock that could not exist, when the cause was contention against a CPUQuota. that is the same instrument confusion with the sign flipped --- a duration trusted as a property of the code. one venue now has both directions measured},
  check    = {concept:report/provenance/friction-a-shared-box-invalidates-durations-and-a-blanket-refusal-discards-the-counters},
  note     = {by=gabion kind=friction}
}

@misc{friction-a-worth-judgement-partitions-a-choice-whose-two-sides-differ-by-precondition,
  section  = {report},
  claim    = {*too coarse for the limit to be worth computing* is a partition disguised as a measurement, and the operator's objection to it is structural rather than stylistic: a WORTH judgement declares one side of a cost/benefit either-or settled while producing no number for either side. it is a green over an empty domain at the level of JUDGEMENT rather than of data --- the same defect as an absence claim whose population enumerated zero files, one level up, and it is invisible for the same reason: a verdict with no denominator is indistinguishable from a verdict with one. --- resolved by finding the common structure under the either-or, recursively, rather than by picking a side. FIRST: a hit rate is not a verdict on a construction, it is a measurement of the SPAN. if the shared object covers 8.4% of the population then 91.6% is remainder, and the construction follows from the ignorance rather than from the subject --- so a coarse span does not make the limit UNWORTHY, it makes the limit INADMISSIBLE, because possessing the selection criterion a limit requires contradicts the premise that made anyone consult the corpus. the two sides are not options with different payoffs; they are constructions with different PRECONDITIONS, and one precondition is measurably absent. SECOND: the figure was doing instrument work and got read as subject work. the complement is not one thing --- it partitions into identified-as-distinct, near-miss-declined-with-a-reason, and shapes-the-reader-returns-empty-for --- and collapsing those three into *coarse* is the absent-versus-unavailable collapse again. THIRD, and it bottoms out here: both constructions need the SAME thing, a per-row witness. over-glue and under-glue are one defect, an unwarranted or a missing identification, so a limit with no witness per row is the apex's convenience and a colimit with no witness per row is a pile. the invariant under the either-or is the witness, and the only operation that improves EITHER construction is raising the witness rate. --- the checkable restatement, with the judgement removed: *the rate is the witness rate of the span, not a verdict on the glue; at a low rate the limit's precondition is absent, so the colimit is not preferred but the only admissible construction, and the remainder is carried partitioned by WHY each row failed to identify.* that partition is the work item, and it is constructible, reachable, observable and coverable --- where *worth* is none of the four. --- adjacent, from paperkit and measured: 114,982 frontier work units against 17,918 executed actions in one run, and *no function of the progress line predicts wall time* because time-to-completion needs the per-run cache hit rate, which the progress stream does not carry. two runs of one target differed 5x in seconds-per-unit. a ratio between two different denominators is not a rate of anything a reader can act on, which is the same error the worth judgement makes with the arithmetic left out},
  check    = {concept:report/provenance/friction-a-worth-judgement-partitions-a-choice-whose-two-sides-differ-by-precondition},
  note     = {by=gabion kind=friction}
}

