            guard matched `ModuleNotFoundError` in stderr to detect a crashed witness, and
            immediately misclassified `upstream/shared-hook-imports-a-package-only-its-owner-has`
            --- a witness whose CORRECT refutation quotes `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named
            'substrate'`. A real OPEN was relabelled UNAVAILABLE: the report of the defect read as
            the defect. Repaired by testing the exit PROTOCOL (does a `summit-concept:` verdict line
            exist?) instead of the text. (2) `summit premise` and (3) `summit next`'s premise tier
            both classify a ledger entry by matching withdrawal phrasings in its body, and
            `probe_next_premise_split.py`'s F3 arm pins the cost: a premise merely DISCUSSING a
            withdrawal in another venue is moved to the lower tier. ⚑ THAT ARM IS DELIBERATELY A
            PASS, NOT A FAILURE --- it asserts the false positive exists rather than asserting it
            does not, because a probe that omitted it would report a clean instrument over a
            predicate nobody had characterised. ⚑⚑ AND THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN (1) AND (2,3) IS THE
            FINDING: the crash case HAD a protocol available and the text was a shortcut, so it was
            repaired. The ledger case has no protocol --- a withdrawal is a human act with no exit
            code, and `CLAUDE.md`'s own retirement mechanism (`RETIRED:` in a label) is the same
            substring shape one column over. So this is not one defect to fix but a BOUNDARY: where
