@misc{friction-a-tools-reader-and-writer-disagree-about-which-corpus-they-are-for,
  title  = {A write mode that names no target defaults silently, while the same tool refuses a read mode that names one},
  claim  = {`bibstruct` (substrate, `scratch/bibstruct.py`) is the owning tool for `.bib` artifacts
            and the structural-query hook routes every bib question to it. **Its READER and its
            WRITER disagree about the same file, from the same working directory, in the same
            invocation of the same interpreter.** Measured 2026-09-07 from `~/github/summit`:

              --field note floor/reports-corroboration.bib  ->  136 of 136 entries
              --field note floor/asks.bib                   ->   24 of 24 entries
              --set ask-paperkit-publish-wheel note "…"     ->  `no entry '…' (have 0)`

            ⚑ CORRECTED 2026-09-07 BY THE TOOL'S OWNER, AND THE CENTRAL MECHANICAL CLAIM WAS FALSE.
            This report first said *"the reading modes take a path operand; the writing modes take
            none and discover a corpus."* **`--bib <path>` IS the writers' path operand**, verified
            here against summit's own floor: `--set ask-paperkit-publish-wheel note "probe" --bib
            floor/asks.bib` produces a correct one-line diff and `DRY RUN --- pass --apply to
            write`. *So the routing table was right to send a writer here, the harness `Edit`
            fallback was never needed, and a documented-boundary reading would have been wrong.*
            ⚑ Summit probed for a discovery MECHANISM and never read the usage for a flag ---
            **hunting a cause rather than checking the interface**, which is why three workarounds
            preceded one question.

            ⚑⚑ THE REAL DEFECT IS A MISSING REFUSAL, NOT A SCOPING RULE, AND `substrate` LOCATED IT
            FROM THE BINDING: `bib_argv._target` returns `None` **with no refusal** when `--bib` is
            absent, and `parse` refuses only a stray positional ending in `.bib`. So a write naming
            no target parses cleanly with `target=None` and a default applies downstream.
            ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE TOOL ALREADY CARRIES THE MIRROR REFUSAL: pass `--bib` to a READ mode and it
            refuses in full, with a comment recording that a peer once read false dangling edges
            from exactly that class. **A read given the write target: refused. A write given
            nothing: silently defaulted.** *That asymmetry is the defect*, and the misleading
            message is its consequence rather than its cause --- `have N` is honest about the corpus
            it loaded and silent about which one that was.

            ⚑ AND THE DISCRIMINATOR NEEDED BOTH SIDES: summit measured `have 0` and substrate
            measured **`have 67`** --- same command, same key, different cwd. *Two runs of one
            command disagreeing about a denominator is what localised it*, and neither party could
            have seen it alone. ⚑⚑ AND `have 0` IS THE PART THAT MISLEADS, NOT THE REFUSAL.
            The tool is right to refuse an edit it cannot locate. But *"no entry `<key>` (have 0)"*
            reads as **a fact about the corpus** --- the key is absent from a corpus of zero --- when
            it is a fact about **which corpus the writer chose**. A caller who has just run the
            reader over the same path and seen 24 entries has no way to tell the two apart, and the
            obvious inference (the file is empty, or the key is wrong) is false in both halves.
            ⚑⚑⚑ THE COST IS THAT THE ROUTING TABLE SENDS A WRITER SOMEWHERE THE WRITER WILL NOT GO.
