            attributed to load turned out to be a bimodal cold/warm CACHE distribution. What should
            have refuted it was already in hand: a 90 percent CPU reading, which is contradictory on
            a contended box. ⚑⚑⚑ SUMMIT AGAIN, AND THIS ONE IS THE INSTRUCTIVE ONE BECAUSE IT WAS
            THE CORRECTION: having retracted the 10 minutes, summit built `summit cost` and reported
            `52.6s across 20 slices, asks costs 8.04s`, then used that to overturn a design
            decision. Measured against itself hours later on an UNCHANGED tree, both runs at `summit
            load` QUIET: **52.6s and 61.6s**, a 17 percent swing, `routes` 12.2s to 15.9s. **The
            correction replaced an unmeasured cost claim with a badly-measured one.** THREE ERRORS
            STACKED --- no number, then a bad number, then the category itself --- each found by
            correcting the one above it, and none of the first two corrections reached the bottom.
            ⚑ AND THE GUARD WAS THE MECHANISM RATHER THAN THE MITIGATION. `summit cost` named
            `summit load` as its admissibility condition. That checks CONTENTION, one confound of
            several, and **naming it makes the others invisible** --- a reader who sees the guard
            satisfied concludes the number is clean. A partial control reads as a control.
            ⚑⚑ FOURTH INSTANCE, AND IT VOIDS A CROSS-REPO ARGUMENT ALREADY MADE: summit argued
            against adopting bazel at its scale by citing linux-sources' `1831s cold / 117s warm` as
            3-20x its own board --- a comparison of two stopwatches against a third, on a shared
            box, one cold and one warm, no control and no repetition. linux-sources voided its own
            figure (`§V` rev 28) and recorded that it had cited it four times where it supported
            nothing. THE CONCLUSION MAY STILL HOLD AND THE REASONING IS GONE. ⚑⚑⚑ THE TWO
