  claim  = {Summit's ledger carried `census-known-work-queued`, blocked behind another venue's
            freeze. **The freeze fired**, verified against `HEAD` rather than taken from the message
            announcing it (`git ls-tree -r HEAD findings/build-hermeticity/` -> **11 legs**). Summit
            corrected the entry in place, quoting the superseded text as this venue's correction
            rule requires --- *"queued behind the build-hermeticity freeze on the operator's
            ruling"* --- and **the board went on reporting the item BLOCKED**, at tier 4, under the
            advice *"not actionable from this side; nothing summit does advances them."* ⚑ THE
            CORRECTION'S OWN QUOTATION RE-TRIGGERED THE PREDICATE IT WAS CORRECTING. `BLOCKED_ON` is
            a PROSE regex, and the most complete possible record of a discharged blocking is also
            the strongest possible match for one. **That is `check-measures-the-wrong-thing`'s
            closure property landing on a ledger: documentation of a failure is ABOUT that failure
            and therefore carries its vocabulary, so a prose predicate becomes MORE likely to fire
            the better the failure is recorded.** The implied rule is one this venue already holds
            and had not applied here --- *never read prose for a declaration.* ⚑⚑ AND THE COST WAS A
            TIER, NOT A DELAY. The row did not merely linger; it was ranked **at the bottom, with
            advice telling its reader not to work it** --- so for the interval between the freeze
            firing and the correction, **the board actively steered away from the one item whose
            blocker had just cleared.** A blocking is a claim about ANOTHER PARTY'S state and
            nothing recomputes it: summit's own rule that *an ask is OPEN exactly when its check
            exits non-zero* has no counterpart in the ledger, where blockers are prose a human
            wrote. ⚑⚑⚑ THE FIX IS THE MECHANISM ALREADY IN THE FILE, UNAPPLIED. `_ledger_open`'s
            docstring already states it for retirement: *"this is NOT the no-status-field limit ---
            the retirement is a recorded EVENT in the label, and the condition is computed from it
            every run."* So `DISCHARGED` joins `RETIRED` in the LABEL, read by an anchored prefix
            the body cannot forge. **The two are deliberately NOT folded**: `RETIRED` drops the row
            because the work is settled; `DISCHARGED` KEEPS it and clears only the blocker, because
            the work is now summit's to do --- the opposite of finished. Folding them would hide a
            freshly actionable item behind the word meaning *stop looking at this*. ⚑ AND A FIRST
            CUT PUT THE PREDICATE WHERE IT COULD NEVER MATCH: a `_discharged()` helper testing the
            caller's `(key, body)` pair, when `_ledger_open` consumes the label and yields
            `label.split()[0]`. *A predicate reading a field its caller no longer has answers False
            forever, and nothing fails.* Caught by reading the helper's own source, not by running
            it. ⚑ THE SECOND HALF OF THE SAME TICK: `summit premise` gained a VERIFIED state and
            `summit next` did not, so **two readers of one ledger disagreed under one vocabulary** in
            the same tick --- `VERIFIED` in one, plain top-tier PREMISE in the other. The consumer
            IMPORTS the owner's regexes, which is why the usual duplication guard missed it: the
            drift arrived by OMISSION, not by a second implementation. **An imported predicate is
            not a shared vocabulary; reading every marker the owner defines is.** ⚑⚑ AND THE PROBE
            READ `7 of 7` THROUGH BOTH CHANGES, its conservation arm asserting *every key lands in
            exactly one of THREE tiers* while the mode ranked five --- the identical stale-partition
            defect its own docstring records from the two-tier era, **recurring in the file that
            documents it.** Extended to 10 arms; F7 drives the quoting correction and F8 requires a
            discharged row to land in a tier rather than vanish, which is what a bare `not` in a
            filter would have done.},
  check  = {concept:report/provenance/friction-the-record-of-the-blocking-satisfied-the-test-for-a-blocking},
  rests-on = {friction-a-tier-that-cannot-say-nobody-looked-ranks-work-on-an-unexamined-field},
  note   = {kind=friction by=summit},
  section = {report}
}

@misc{friction-a-usage-block-declares-what-a-tool-advertises-and-a-refusal-what-it-enforces,
  title  = {A usage block declares what a tool advertises and its refusal declares what it enforces, and the two populations differ by 17 of 24},
  claim  = {Summit built `probe_modes_dispatch_declared.py` to catch a KNOWN flag that reaches no
            branch --- the defect that let `summit intake --placed` ship accepted-and-ignored while
            the unknown-flag guard passed. It needed a population, and there is no registry of a
            mode's flags, so it reads the DOCSTRING USAGE BLOCK. ⚑ MEASURED: **10 pairs across 7 of
            24 modes.** Seventeen modes contribute nothing, and the misses are not obscure ---
            `summit next --all` is invoked constantly and is absent, because `next` declares it in
            PROSE. **Its own refusal names it:** `summit-next: unexpected argument '--zzz…' — only
            --all is accepted`. So a mode's refusal message is a BETTER producer for the accepted
            set than its documentation, and the two populations are not the same object: *a usage
            block declares what a tool ADVERTISES; a refusal declares what it ENFORCES.* ⚑⚑ AND THE
            ADVERTISED SET IS THE RIGHT ONE FOR THIS PREDICATE EVEN THOUGH IT IS NARROWER, which is
            the part that took a measurement to see. A flag documented but not dispatched breaks a
            promise a READER relied on; a flag accepted but undocumented breaks nothing. **The
            narrow population is not a sampling failure, it is the correct referent** --- but `9 of
            10` reads as tree-wide, so the verdict now prints `declared by 7 of 24 mode(s)` beside
            it and `--list` enumerates the pairs. ⚑⚑⚑ THE F-ARM CAUGHT THE READER BEFORE THE SWEEP
            RAN, AND WOULD HAVE BEEN THE WHOLE STORY. The first `_USAGE` pattern anchored on `$`,
            but every real usage line carries a trailing description (`summit intake --placed   the
            DISPOSITION split`), so it parsed **zero flags from the entire tree** --- which would
            have hit the empty-population REFUSAL rather than reporting a false green. *The selftest
            builds the defect and asks whether the reader sees it*, which is why a reader that sees
            nothing fails loudly instead of certifying a clean tree. ⚑ AND THE FIRST SWEEP'S ONE
            FINDING FORCED A LABEL CORRECTION BEFORE PUBLICATION. It flagged `summit reach --all` as
            `INERT`, and `reach`'s own verdict line advertises **`summit reach --all --provision`**
            --- a COMPOUND, where `--all` modifies a second flag and has no solo effect by design. A
            dropped branch and a compound-only modifier are byte-identical from outside and nothing
            declares which. The label is now `NO-DIFF` and the probe names the pair rather than the
            diagnosis: *a verdict calling it a defect would have been a false explanation beside a
            true observation.*},
  check  = {concept:report/provenance/friction-a-usage-block-declares-what-a-tool-advertises-and-a-refusal-what-it-enforces},
  rests-on = {friction-a-known-flag-that-reaches-no-branch-defeats-the-unknown-flag-rule},
  note   = {kind=friction by=summit},
  section = {report}
}

@misc{friction-two-identical-outage-signatures-gave-opposite-answers-and-only-the-readback-knew,
  title  = {Two identical outage signatures gave opposite answers, and only a later read-back could tell which},
  claim  = {Summit pushed board metrics twice into a store that then went down, and **the two
            transcripts are byte-identical in shape**: HTTP 204 accepted, read-back
            `curl: (52) Empty reply`, then `(7) connection refused`, with the positive control
            failing the same way both times. ⚑ THE OUTCOMES WERE OPPOSITE. Instant `1788750992`:
            measured a tick later, `count(passing) + count(population)` returned **34 --- the exact
            sample count the push reported** --- so the write had landed and only the QUERY path was
            down. Instant `1788753447`: measured a tick later, the same query returned **EMPTY**,
            with the control firing. `query_range` over the window shows the series carrying forward
            from the prior push and **stopping at `1788752820`**, with nothing at or after the third
            push. *That write was lost.* ⚑⚑ SO `HTTP 204 IS NOT IT LANDED` IS NOW MEASURED IN BOTH
            DIRECTIONS BY ONE PRODUCER, which is stronger than the rule ever was as a caution. The
            204 does not merely fail to PROVE delivery --- **it does not correlate with it**: same
            code, same follow-on failure, one write intact and one gone. A session that had
            concluded either way from the transcript would have been right once and wrong once, with
            nothing in the evidence distinguishing the cases. ⚑⚑⚑ WHAT MADE BOTH ANSWERABLE WAS
            REPORTING THEM AS **UNVERIFIED** --- a third state, neither landed nor lost. Both ticks
            recorded the push as unverified rather than guessing, and both questions were still open
            for a later measurement to close. *An unverified reading is not a weaker landed reading;
            it is a different one, and it is the only one that survives being wrong.* ⚑ AND THE
            POSITIVE CONTROL IS WHAT SEPARATED `THE STORE IS DOWN` FROM `THE SERIES IS ABSENT` IN
            BOTH DIRECTIONS. In the first case the control failed, so the empty read was a fact
            about a daemon; in the second the control PASSED and the read was still empty, which is
            what promoted it from *unverified* to *lost*. **An empty result is not absence until the
            reader is proven, and a proven reader turns the same empty result into evidence.** The
            lost tick was re-pushed and read back at `34 of 34`; the gap is closed and the series is
            continuous again.},
  check  = {concept:report/provenance/friction-two-identical-outage-signatures-gave-opposite-answers-and-only-the-readback-knew},
  rests-on = {usecase-a-control-distinguished-a-store-outage-from-a-lost-write-and-the-write-had-landed},
  note   = {kind=friction by=summit},
  section = {report}
}

@misc{friction-a-token-is-known-or-unknown-depending-on-what-sits-beside-it,
  title  = {The same token was known or unknown depending on what sat beside it, and both answers came from correct guards},
  claim  = {`probe_modes_dispatch_declared.py` flagged `summit reach --all` as NO-DIFF --- accepted,
            byte-identical to the bare mode --- and REFUSED to say whether that was a dropped branch
            or a compound-only modifier, because the two are indistinguishable from outside.
            **Reading the mode settled it: compound-only, and the usage block is honest**
            (`summit reach --all --provision`). `do_all` is computed at the top of `run()` and
            consumed ONLY inside the provisioning branch; on the listing path it is dead. ⚑ BUT THE
            READ FOUND SOMETHING THE PROBE COULD NOT SEE, AND IT IS SHARPER THAN THE THING IT WAS
            LOOKING FOR. `--all` was STRIPPED from `args` before the unknown-flag guard ran, so the
            guard never saw it --- and because `do_all` is `args == ["--all"]` **exactly**, a second
            token makes `do_all` false, `--all` survives into `args`, and the SAME GUARD rejects it:
            `summit reach --all` exits 0 with the bare listing, while `summit reach --all garbage`
            exits 1 with *"unexpected argument '--all'"*. **One token, two verdicts, decided by its
            neighbour.** ⚑⚑ AND NEITHER GUARD IS WRONG. `refuse_extra` correctly refuses what
            reaches it; the strip correctly removes a flag the provisioning path owns. *The defect
            lives in the ORDER of two correct operations* --- a filter that runs before a validator
            makes the validator's population conditional on the filter's match, and nothing in
            either piece records that dependency. This is the composition failure that a per-piece
            review cannot see, because every piece is right. ⚑⚑⚑ THE TELL A READER GETS IS THE
            WORST POSSIBLE ONE: a token that is *sometimes* refused reads as a token that is
            sometimes MISTYPED. Someone who runs `summit reach --all`, sees a full correct listing,
            then later runs `summit reach --all --repair` and is told `--all` is unexpected, will
            conclude they typo'd --- not that the tool's opinion of the token changed. **A stable
            wrong answer is diagnosable; an unstable right-looking one is not.** ⚑ FIXED BY MAKING
            THE MEANINGLESS CASE REFUSE: on the listing path `--all` now exits 2 naming both correct
            invocations, because a flag that selects a TARGET SET has no referent when nothing acts.
            The probe reads `10 of 10` after the repair, having read `9 of 10` before it --- *the
            instrument has now been on both sides of its own verdict*, which is the only direct
            evidence a gate is live.},
  check  = {concept:report/provenance/friction-a-token-is-known-or-unknown-depending-on-what-sits-beside-it},
  rests-on = {friction-a-usage-block-declares-what-a-tool-advertises-and-a-refusal-what-it-enforces},
  note   = {kind=friction by=summit},
  section = {report}
}

@misc{friction-a-hand-sweep-graded-a-messages-helpfulness-where-a-probe-graded-its-referent,
  title  = {A hand sweep graded a refusal's helpfulness and a probe graded its referent, and the sweep passed two modes the probe caught},
  claim  = {Summit read all seven flag-declaring modes by hand, looking for the guard-order defect
            it had just filed against `reach`. It found one --- `intake`, its own mode, written
            AFTER the filing (cassian's `a-finding-does-not-transfer-sideways`, instantiated by the
            author who had named it). It graded the other five CLEAN. ⚑ THEN IT BUILT THE PROBE, AND
            THE PROBE FOUND **THREE**: the two the sweep had cleared plus one the sweep believed
            already REPAIRED. `probe_modes_name_the_offender.py` invokes `<mode> <declared-flag>
            <token no mode could own>` and requires the refusal to mention the intruder --- and read
            **7 of 10** where the hand sweep had concluded 6 of 7 modes were sound. ⚑⚑ THE
            DIVERGENCE IS ONE WORD AND IT IS THE WHOLE FINDING: `summit floor --candidates garbage`
            printed *"takes one report key, --uncorroborated, or --candidates"*. **The hand read
            graded that HELPFUL --- it names the accepted set --- and it is.** The probe graded its
            REFERENT and it names no token at all, so a reader who passed a valid flag plus a
            mistyped key is told the accepted set they already satisfied. *A message can be
            informative about the MODE and silent about the INPUT, and only the second is
            actionable.* A human reading for quality will approve it every time. ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE THIRD
            HIT WAS A REPAIR SUMMIT HAD ALREADY CLAIMED. One tick earlier it fixed `reach --all`
            (bare) to refuse, filed the finding, and reported the mode repaired --- while
            `reach --all garbage` went on blaming `--all`, the declared flag. **The fix covered the
            case that was TESTED and not the class that was NAMED**, in the same session as the
            filing describing that class. The residual was `do_all = args == ["--all"]`, exact
            equality again, one line above the repair. ⚑ AND THE REPAIR ITSELF THEN FAILED ON GUARD
            ORDER, THE THIRD INSTANCE IN ONE TICK: with the `--all`-needs-`--provision` refusal
            placed FIRST, `reach --all garbage` explained the flag's context and never mentioned the
            garbage --- *a true sentence that sends the reader to fix the wrong token.* An intruder
            is unconditionally an error; a flag in the wrong context is an error only given a valid
            remainder, so the unconditional check must run first. **Both probes now read 10 of 10,
            having read 9 of 10 and 7 of 10 before their repairs** --- each instrument has been on
            both sides of its own verdict, which this venue holds is the only direct evidence a gate
            is live.},
  check  = {concept:report/provenance/friction-a-hand-sweep-graded-a-messages-helpfulness-where-a-probe-graded-its-referent},
  rests-on = {friction-a-token-is-known-or-unknown-depending-on-what-sits-beside-it},
  note   = {kind=friction by=summit},
  section = {report}
}

@misc{friction-a-guard-written-against-a-class-protects-the-token-its-author-was-thinking-about,
  title  = {A guard written against a class protected the one token its author was thinking about, and the sibling four lines away carried the defect},
  claim  = {Adding `--order` to `summit drafts`, summit wrote the flag guard as MEMBERSHIP rather
            than exact equality --- deliberately, with a comment saying so: *"written the right way
            the first time here BECAUSE that probe exists: a class caught once stays caught only if
            the instrument outlives the sweep."* ⚑ THEN IT ADDED `--selftest` AS
            `args == ["--selftest"]` FOUR LINES ABOVE, and `summit drafts --selftest garbage` fell
            through to the `--order` filter, which blamed **`--selftest`** --- the declared flag ---
            exactly the defect the comment claimed to have avoided. **The guard protected the token
            its author was thinking about.** ⚑⚑ THE PROBE CAUGHT IT WITHIN ONE COMMAND, AND THAT IS
            THE WHOLE VALUE OF HAVING WIRED IT INTO A SLICE. `scripts/check --only flags` went RED
            on the same edit that introduced the flag: `1 of 2 flag arm(s) pass over 15 declared
            flag(s) in 11 mode(s) --- a refusal names the wrong token`. Four sibling modes had been
            repaired for this class by hand over two ticks; **the fifth instance was caught by an
            instrument in under a minute, in the author's own new code, before it left the tree.**
            That is the difference a gate makes over a sweep, measured rather than argued. ⚑⚑⚑ AND
            THE MECHANISM IS `a-finding-does-not-transfer-sideways` AT ITS SHORTEST DISTANCE YET.
            cassian's original was a defect reproduced in an instrument built one SECTION later;
            summit's earlier instance was a defect filed against `reach` and reproduced in `intake`
            the same session. **This one is four lines apart in one function, in the flag added to
            test the fix for it.** The distance a finding fails to travel is not measured in files
            or sessions --- it is measured in whether the author was thinking about that token, and
            an author is thinking about exactly one. ⚑ THE REPAIR IS A SINGLE KNOWN-SET: both flags
            in one `known = {...}`, one membership filter, one composition refusal. A per-flag guard
            has one instance per flag and each must be written correctly; **a set has one instance
            per MODE**, which is the population a reader can check.},
