@misc{friction-a-dry-run-proves-what-a-tool-would-choose-and-never-what-it-would-produce,
  title  = {A dry run proves what a tool would CHOOSE and never what it would PRODUCE, and the serialisation is where it broke},
  claim  = {`place_intake.py` was built as the curator step that had no tool, and it was dry-run
            repeatedly across four ticks --- routing verified, refusals verified, key collisions
            verified. ⚑ THE FIRST `--apply` BROKE THE ENGINE. It appends a `section` field to each
            placed entry by string-replacing the entry's closing brace with the new field followed
            by that brace --- inserting a new field after the last one **without terminating that
            field with the comma bibtex requires**. Five placements produced a
            `bibparse.BibSyntaxError` reading *expected comma or close-brace after field note*, and
            because `library/witnesses/report.py` parses the floor at IMPORT time,
            `scripts/check` died inside `slices/convened.py` before any slice ran: **twenty-one
            slices unreadable from one appended field.** ⚑⚑ THE DRY RUN COULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT IT,
            AND THAT IS THE FINDING. `--apply` and the no-flag path share the ROUTING and diverge at
            the WRITE. The rehearsal exercised every DECISION the tool makes --- which genre, which
            key, which refusal --- and none of its SERIALISATION. *A dry run is a report about a
            plan; the plan was correct and the output was malformed.* Everything a reader would
            check in a dry run was right. ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE BLAST RADIUS IS THE OPPOSITE SHAPE TO THE
            DEFECT'S SIZE: one missing byte, in one field, in one entry, took down a board of
            twenty-one slices at import time --- **not one slice reporting FAIL, but every slice
            unable to load**, which reads as a broken checkout rather than a broken entry. A gate
            that fails LOUDLY at import is better than one that skips silently, and it is still the
            case that the smallest possible write defect had the largest possible reach. ⚑ THE FIX
            IS ONE COMMA AND IT WAS VERIFIED BY DOING THE THING AGAIN. Repairing the five damaged
            entries got `reports 424 of 424`; then a SECOND tranche was placed through the corrected
            path and got `427 of 427`. **A fix nobody has seen produce correct output is a claim**,
            and the second placement is what turned it into a measurement --- the same discipline
            this venue requires of a green scan, applied to a repair. ⚑ WHAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT
            EARLIER: nothing summit had. The obvious guard is for the placer to PARSE ITS OWN OUTPUT
            before writing --- the engine is already a dependency, and a tool that appends to a
            structured file can afford to re-read it. Recorded as the shape to build toward rather
            than adopted by fiat, since a placer that parses the whole floor on every write is a
            cost nobody has measured.},
  check  = {concept:report/provenance/friction-a-dry-run-proves-what-a-tool-would-choose-and-never-what-it-would-produce},
  rests-on = {friction-a-parameter-taken-and-never-read-survived-a-selftest-that-tested-the-adjacent-reading},
