  claim  = {Summit filed `friction-a-dry-run-proves-what-a-tool-would-choose-and-never-what-it-would-
            produce` --- a finding about a placement tool emitting malformed bib --- and **placing it
            emitted malformed bib.** The claim quoted the defective code and the resulting parser
            error verbatim, including an UNMATCHED closing brace inside a string literal and a bare
            close-brace inside backticks. The bib parser has no string layer: it counts braces, so
            the field terminated early and `bibparse.BibSyntaxError: expected comma or close-brace
            after field claim` took the whole board down at import time --- **the second time in two
            ticks that one entry stopped twenty-one slices from loading.** ⚑ BACKTICKS ARE NOT
            ESCAPING, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE MECHANISM. This venue already records that *"backticks do
            not help: the parser splits the whole note on whitespace, so quoting is not
            tokenisation"* --- stated for the `by=`/`of=` tail. **The identical fact holds for
            BRACES in a `claim`,** and nothing said so, because the tail rule was written about one
            field and read as being about that field. *A rule discovered on one surface stops at that
            surface unless someone states the invariant.* ⚑⚑ AND THE CONTENT IS WHAT MADE IT
            LIKELY, NOT UNLUCKY. A finding about a serialisation defect NEEDS to quote the
            serialisation; the more precisely a claim describes broken syntax, the more likely it is
