@misc{friction-a-tautological-assertion-is-worse-than-a-mute-one-and-a-mute-hunt-is-what-found-it,
  title  = {A hunt for assertions that print too little found one that measures nothing},
  claim  = {Summit gates its witness families on whether a failing assertion PRINTS the components
            of what it asserts — a mute refusal cannot distinguish a bad artifact from a bad
            instrument. Repairing `library/witnesses/registry.py` under that rule surfaced a
            different defect the rule was not looking for: `assert isinstance(unknown, list)`, over
            a value built by a LIST COMPREHENSION. ⚑ **A TAUTOLOGY IS WORSE THAN A MUTE
            ASSERTION.** A mute one tells a reader too little when it fires; a tautological one
            **can never fire**, and it reads as certifying the property in its neighbourhood — here,
            that `--unknown-to` is computable for every delegate — while measuring nothing at all.
            *The mute-assertion probe flagged it only because a tautology also has nothing to
            print*, which is an accident of the two defects overlapping and not a property of the
            instrument. ⚑⚑ THE REPLACEMENT HAD TO ASSERT WHAT THE LOOP ALWAYS CLAIMED, WHICH THE
            TAUTOLOGY OBSCURED: the per-delegate unknown-sets must not be IDENTICAL across
            delegates. A query returning the same set for everyone is reporting the REGISTRY'S SIZE
            rather than any delegate's discovery gap — the population-decides-its-own-norm defect,
            appearing in a query rather than in a count. `isinstance` held perfectly in exactly that
            broken state. ⚑⚑⚑ AND A NEW ASSERTION THAT PASSES PROVES NOTHING UNTIL IT IS FORCED TO
            FAIL. Swapping a tautology for an assertion nobody has ever seen refuse is swapping one
            uninformative green for another, so both replacements are forced in
            `scripts/probe_registry_witness_arms.py` — an empty delegate roster, and every
            capability uncited so all delegates share one set. **2 of 2 fire; the tautology could
            fire on neither.** ⚑ THE PROBE'S OWN FIRST RUN CRASHED WITH `RecursionError`: its stub
            read `loader.load_capabilities` from inside the patch, resolving to the patch. A stub
            that calls through must HOLD the original rather than look it up — captured at import
            and recorded in the file, because the failure is silent until the stub is exercised, and
            a stub nobody exercises is the same class as the tautology it was written to refute.},
  check  = {concept:report/provenance/friction-a-tautological-assertion-is-worse-than-a-mute-one-and-a-mute-hunt-is-what-found-it},
  rests-on = {friction-a-rule-that-constrains-its-output-and-not-its-input},
  note   = {kind=friction by=summit},
  section = {report}
}

