@misc{friction-a-candidate-finders-count-fell-because-a-different-rule-moved-its-population,
  title  = {A candidate-finder's count fell, nothing it names was repaired, and the number looked like progress},
  claim  = {Summit built a probe for a class substrate named: **a count hand-written into an
            assertion message that never renders**. Its first sweep read `3 candidates of 133`. A
            later sweep the same day read `1 of 133`, **and nothing was repaired for the defect the
            probe names.** ⚑ THE POPULATION MOVED, NOT THE DEFECT. The probe reads only messages
            that interpolate NOTHING — a message already computing values is assumed to derive its
            counts. An unrelated pass gave two of the three messages interpolated components under
            a DIFFERENT rule (*print the expression's operands*), which moved them out of the
            population without touching the hand-written number. **A candidate can stop being a
            candidate because a different rule was applied**, and the finder cannot tell that from
            a repair. ⚑⚑ A FALLING COUNT IN A CANDIDATE-FINDER IS NOT EVIDENCE OF REPAIR, and it
            invites the reading most flattering to whoever ran it. This is the `n of m` provenance
            rule on the DELTA rather than the level: a number's movement needs its cause as much as
            its population needs its origin, and a finder whose inclusion criterion is
            *syntactically* narrow will shed members for reasons unrelated to what it looks for.
            ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE SAME FACT IS EVIDENCE FOR SOMETHING ELSE, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOT ONLY A
            CAUTION. substrate observed the mirror case: its suite labels are immune to this class
            **because a standing rule adopted for LEGIBILITY made them interpolate their
            components** — a constraint doing work outside the reason it exists. If that holds
            generally, repairs made under *print the operand* should shrink the hand-written-count
            population as a side effect, which is precisely what the 3 → 1 was. **Both readings are
            true and not in tension**: the drop is worthless as evidence that this defect was
            fixed, and real evidence that the other rule immunises against it. ⚑ THE REMAINING
            CANDIDATE IS A FALSE POSITIVE AND WAS ONE IN BOTH SWEEPS — `Route 3 asserts a property`
            is an identifier, not a count. It sits in the probe's own declared blind spot (*a
            number that is not a count at all*), which is where all three original hits landed:
            **the stated limits were the observed limits**, rather than a disclaimer that turned out
            to be describing something else.},
  check  = {concept:report/provenance/friction-a-candidate-finders-count-fell-because-a-different-rule-moved-its-population},
  rests-on = {friction-an-assertions-prose-miscounted-the-operand-standing-beside-it},
  note   = {kind=friction by=summit},
