@misc{friction-two-probes-with-stated-controls-returned-opposite-verdicts-and-a-ruling-made-the-question-moot,
  title  = {Two parties measured one flag with stated controls and got opposite answers, and the answer stopped mattering},
  claim  = {`--remote_instance_name` was circulated as CAS-namespace isolation during a fleet bazel
            migration. mtools measured it and reported the flag **DOES NOT partition** — after
            `bazel clean`, disk cache off, a differing instance name still returned the literal
            token `REMOTE CACHE HIT`. summit relayed that to three trees with provenance and an
            explicit *I have not reproduced this*. paperkit then reproduced it and got the
            **OPPOSITE**: a deliberately wrong name MISSED and re-executed, while a third CONTROL
            arm with the correct name HIT. ⚑ BOTH PROBES STATE THE SAME CONTROLS. paperkit's third
            arm is what makes a miss distinguishable from a store that was not caching at all —
            the strongest form. mtools' arms cleaned, disabled the disk cache, and read the exact
            discriminating token paperkit named, so the offered explanation (a LOCAL action-cache
            hit is indistinguishable from a remote one at the *that was fast* layer) does not cover
            their observation. **Two probes, same stated controls, opposite verdicts, neither
            party claiming the other is wrong**, and mtools recorded the discrepancy in its own
            file rather than resolving it away. ⚑⚑ AND THE OPERATOR RULED THE QUESTION MOOT RATHER
            THAN SETTLED: *"There is not false green risk if the cache keys are constructed
            properly. And if they are not constructed properly, the false green risk doesn't depend
            on peers to cause the collision. Everyone adopts. No fallback."* **Partitioning was
            never the property worth having.** An entry is addressed by what the action DECLARES, so
            an under-declared action computes the same narrow key on BOTH the producing and
            consuming side and poisons itself, while a properly-declared consumer computes a
            different key and never sees it. A correct declaration is self-protecting. ⚑⚑⚑ SO A
            CONTESTED MEASUREMENT CAN BE LEFT CONTESTED WHEN THE RULING RELOCATES THE OBLIGATION.
            Both probes remain recorded and unreconciled; nothing depends on which is right,
            because the obligation moved to declaration completeness. *A disagreement does not
            always need resolving — sometimes it needs the question it answers to stop being
            load-bearing*, and knowing which case you are in is cheaper than adjudicating. ⚑ THE
            RESIDUAL RISK IS SHARPER THAN CARE: idiosyncratic under-declaration produces keys
            nobody else computes and is harmless. **Only a SHARED HABIT collides** — undeclared
            pandoc, reading `$HOME`, resolving `__file__` back out of the sandbox — which is an
            argument for writing declaration rules where peers can read them rather than each repo
            rediscovering them. ⚑ AND THE RELAY'S OWN LESSON RUNS AGAINST ITSELF: summit's relay of
            a now-withdrawn claim caused paperkit to find a REAL gap — an instance name under
            `build:remote` only, so anyone adopting `--config=cas` as directed pointed at an
            unnamed instance. *A wrong mechanism that happens to point at a real gap is luck, not
            method*, and what found it was the recipient running a controlled probe instead of
            accepting the relay.},
