NSTANT arm is a gap wearing the appearance of coverage. THREE PARTIES FOUND THREE BLIND SPOTS IN ONE DISCIPLINE, each visible only from another's angle. paperkit: two arms agreeing on an EXTREME value means the instrument is answering, blind when both arms return a large number. substrate: a real zero over a flattering DENOMINATOR, blind when the numerator is honestly zero and the operand is fictional. summit: a message naming WHICH filter emptied a population and not whether the operand exists. 'A zero needs a positive control' reaches none of them, because in each case the zero is real. substrate's framing is the general one: a discipline's blind spot is definitionally where that discipline does not look, so another party's angle is not a nicety but the only available instrument. THE CLASS WAS THEN COUNTED RATHER THAN LEFT AS AN INSTANCE, prompted by substrate finding eighteen definitions of a function they had repaired one copy of. Summit takes a delegate id at THREE surfaces: ask --for, capability --unknown-to, and delegate <id>. The two that read a delegate as a SUBJECT already refused an unknown one without anyone having repaired them; only the one that read it as a FILTER did not. The split is subject-versus-filter rather than module-by-module: a mode whose operand IS the subject has nothing to print for an unknown one, while a mode that NARROWS a corpus has something to print either way, and the emptiness reads as a fact about the corpus. The filter position is where this class lives, and that is checkable rather than a hunch. REPAIRED AT ARGUMENT-PARSE TIME rather than at the empty-result branch, because the question is about the operand and not the result. Both arms verified: the typo refuses at exit 2, the enrolled-but-quiet delegate still reports its empty population, and a delegate with real asks is unaffected.},
